sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/maynardsmith-ess> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>, <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/TestableHypothesis>;
    dc:description "A strategy is evolutionarily stable if, once common, it cannot be invaded by any rare alternative strategy.";
    dc:source "John Maynard Smith (1973) The Logic of Animal Conflict";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Evolutionarily stable strategy conditions";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://doi.org/10.1016/0025-5564(78)90077-9>;
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "I is an ESS iff for all J \\ne I: E(I,I) > E(J,I), or E(I,I) = E(J,I) and E(I,J) > E(J,J); every ESS is an asymptotically stable rest point of the replicator dynamics (Taylor and Jonker 1978), not conversely";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Assumptions: an infinite, well-mixed population with pairwise interactions and asexual or clonal inheritance of strategies. Formal status: the definition is exact, and the link to replicator dynamics is proved. Finite populations, mutation and genetic constraints can stop an ESS from being reached.";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hypothesisType> "model-theorem";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/proposedTest> "In a system with measurable payoffs (microbial public-goods strains, contest behaviour), estimate the payoff matrix and test that the predicted ESS composition resists experimental invasion by rare alternative strategies."
}
